        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Augustine of Hippo</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">354 CE – 430 CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Born</span><span class="info-stat-val">13 November 354 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thagaste">Thagaste</a>, in the Roman province of Numidia (now Souk Ahras, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>); son of Patricius, a pagan who converted on his deathbed, and Monica, a devout Christian</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Died</span><span class="info-stat-val">28 August 430 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippo-regius">Hippo Regius</a> (now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annaba">Annaba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>), during his final illness while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> besieged the city</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Who they were</span><span class="info-stat-val">Teacher of rhetoric at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thagaste">Thagaste</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> (373–386); priest at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippo-regius">Hippo Regius</a> (from 391); coadjutor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> (395) and then Bishop of Hippo; one of the four Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-christendom">Latin Church</a> Fathers; a saint in the Catholic, Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a>, Lutheran and Anglican traditions</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">the Confessions, often called the first Western autobiography; The City of God; On the Trinity; his teaching on grace, original sin and predestination; his arguments against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donatism">Donatists</a>; just war theory; his conversion in 386</span></div>
