    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thagaste">Thagaste</a> to a pagan father and a Christian mother, Augustine became a teacher of rhetoric, spent years among the Manichaeans and was baptised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambrose">Ambrose</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> in 387.</li><li>As priest and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Hippo from 391 and 395, he wrote the Confessions and The City of God, and argued against the Manichaeans, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donatism">Donatists</a> and the Pelagians.</li><li>He supported the state's use of coercion against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donatism">Donatists</a>, a position his modern defenders and detractors have found troubling, and he died in 430 while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> besieged Hippo.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Augustine never mastered Greek: his first Greek teacher beat his students, and Augustine rebelled and refused to study. By the time he realised he needed it, it was too late, though he became a master of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

