    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">354</span><span class="rf-text">Born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thagaste">Thagaste</a> in Numidia, to a pagan father and a Christian mother</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">384</span><span class="rf-text">Takes up the post of professor of rhetoric at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, at thirty years old</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">386</span><span class="rf-text">Converts to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> after hearing a child's voice say &quot;take up and read&quot;</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">387</span><span class="rf-text">Baptised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambrose">Ambrose</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a> Vigil, together with his son Adeodatus</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">391</span><span class="rf-text">Ordained a priest at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippo-regius">Hippo Regius</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">395</span><span class="rf-text">Made coadjutor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Hippo, and soon full bishop</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">397</span><span class="rf-text">Begins writing the Confessions, completed in 398</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">430</span><span class="rf-text">Dies on 28 August as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> besiege Hippo</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cicero"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cicero.jpg" alt="Cicero" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Cicero_(1st-cent._BC)_-_Palazzo_Nuovo_-_Musei_Capitolini_-_Rome_2016.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">José Luiz, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Augustine was born on 13 November 354 in the municipium of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thagaste">Thagaste</a>, now Souk Ahras in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>, in the Roman province of Numidia. His mother Monica was a devout Christian; his father Patricius was a pagan who converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> on his deathbed. He had a brother, Navigius, and a sister whose name is lost. Scholars generally agree that the family were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a>, heavily Romanised and speaking only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> at home, and in his writings Augustine mentions in passing his identity as a Roman African.</p><p>At eleven he was sent to school at Madaurus, where he became familiar with Latin literature and with pagan beliefs and practices. In the Confessions he tells how he and some friends stole pears from a neighbour's garden, though he was neither hungry nor poor, and how he later brooded over the theft. At seventeen he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> to study rhetoric, through the generosity of his fellow citizen Romanianus, since the cost was beyond his family's means.</p><p>At Carthage he read <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a>'s dialogue Hortensius, now lost, which he said kindled in him the love of wisdom. Raised a Christian, he became a Manichaean, much to his mother's chagrin. He began a relationship with a young woman that lasted more than fifteen years and produced a son, Adeodatus, born in 372. He never mastered Greek.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Augustine of Hippo&#8221; cites where it covers Childhood and education:</p><ul><li>Miles Hollingworth, Saint Augustine of Hippo: An Intellectual Biography (Oxford University Press, 2013)</li><li>Michael Brett, The Berbers (Blackwell, 1996)</li><li>Peter Brown, Augustine of Hippo: A Biography (University of California Press, 2000)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ambrose"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ambrose.jpg" alt="Ambrose" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ambrose">Ambrose</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AmbroseOfMilan_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Augustine taught grammar at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thagaste">Thagaste</a> in 373–374, then ran a school of rhetoric at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> for nine years. Disturbed by unruly students, he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 383, hoping for better ones, but was disappointed: many students attended all term and then did not pay. Manichaean friends introduced him to Symmachus, the prefect of the City of Rome, who had been asked to find a rhetoric professor for the imperial court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. Augustine won the post and took it up in late 384, at thirty, the most visible academic position in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> world.</p><p>He had been a Manichaean auditor, the lowest rank, and a disappointing meeting with the Manichaean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> Faustus of Mileve had begun his doubts. At Milan his mother's piety, his own reading of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoplatonism">Neoplatonists</a> and his friend Simplicianus drew him towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, and he came to know <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambrose">Ambrose</a>, whom he first approached for his reputation as an orator.</p><p>Monica arranged a respectable marriage for him. Augustine had to send away his concubine and grieved for her; he recalled praying "Grant me chastity and continence, but not yet." In late August 386, at thirty-one, after hearing of Ponticianus's reading of the life of Anthony of the Desert, he heard a child's voice say "take up and read" and opened Paul's letters at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> 13:13–14. Ambrose baptised him and Adeodatus in Milan on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a> Vigil, 24–25 April 387.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 388 Augustine and Adeodatus returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. Monica died at Ostia as they prepared to embark, and Adeodatus died soon after. Augustine sold his inheritance and gave the money to the poor, keeping only the family house, which he made a monastic foundation for himself and friends. In 391 he was ordained a priest at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippo-regius">Hippo Regius</a>, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annaba">Annaba</a>, and in 395 he became coadjutor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Hippo and soon full bishop. He turned away from his profession as a teacher of rhetoric to preaching; more than 350 preserved sermons are believed authentic, and his sermons were taken down by stenographers.</p><p>He wrote the Confessions in 397–398. The City of God, in twenty-two books, was written to console fellow Christians after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 410. His other works include On the Trinity, On Christian Doctrine and On Free Choice of the Will, and in his last years the Retractationes, a review of his earlier writings. The list of his works runs to more than one hundred titles.</p><p>Against the Pelagians he taught that the sin of Adam and Eve wounded the human will and that unmerited grace was necessary for salvation. Against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donatism">Donatists</a> he held that the validity of a sacrament does not depend on the holiness of the priest who performs it. In The City of God he coined the phrase "just war", arguing that war must be defensive and authorised by a legitimate authority.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Augustine of Hippo&#8221; cites where it covers Astrology, Epistemology, Just war theory, Free will:</p><ul><li>Kenneth M. Wilson, Augustine's Conversion from Traditional Free Choice to &quot;Non-free Free Will&quot;: A Comprehensive Methodology (Mohr Siebeck, 2018)</li><li>Gareth B. Matthews, Thought's Ego in Augustine and Descartes (Cornell University Press, 1992)</li><li>George Howie, Educational theory and practice in St. Augustine (Routledge and Kegan Paul, 1969)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donatism">Donatists</a>, who refused to readmit clergy who had handed over sacred books under the Great Persecution, and the Catholics had stood side by side for decades, often with two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> in one city. Donatists and Circumcellions carried out street violence, and in Letter 28, around 416, Augustine described Donatists cutting out the tongue and cutting off the hands of Bishop Rogatus, who had converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>. Augustine first held that conversion must be voluntary and appealed in words, then supported the state's use of coercion. In Letter 93, written in 408, he said he changed his mind because of "the ineffectiveness of dialogue and the proven efficacy of laws."</p><p>He recommended fines, imprisonment, banishment and moderate floggings, and opposed maiming and the execution of heretics, though Roman authorities mostly ignored these limits. Herbert Deane found a fundamental inconsistency between this position and Augustine's political thought; Peter Brown called it more an attitude than a doctrine; Frederick Russell argued that it answered a unique historical situation and that later writers applied it far beyond it. Its critics have called him the prince and patriarch of persecutors.</p><p>The Pelagians denied that original sin wounded the human will. Augustine held that the sin of Adam is inherited by all and was the first to add the concept of inherited guilt. Patristics scholar Ken Wilson argues that he abandoned the free choice earlier Christians taught; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> holds that his teaching is consistent with free will.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/pope-boniface-viii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-boniface-viii.jpg" alt="Pope Boniface VIII" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/pope-boniface-viii">Pope Boniface VIII</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giotto_-_Bonifatius_VIII_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Giotto, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Shortly before Augustine's death the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a>, a Germanic tribe that had converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arianism">Arianism</a>, invaded Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. They besieged Hippo in the spring of 430, as Augustine entered his final illness. According to his friend and biographer Possidius, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guelma">Calama</a>, one of the few miracles attributed to Augustine, the healing of an ill man, took place during the siege.</p><p>He spent his final days in prayer and repentance, and asked that the penitential Psalms be hung on his walls so that he could read them; Possidius says he wept freely and constantly. He directed that the library of the church in Hippo be carefully preserved. He died on 28 August 430. The Vandals lifted the siege soon after, but returned and burned the city, destroying all but Augustine's cathedral and library.</p><p>Augustine was canonised by popular acclaim and recognised as a Doctor of the Church by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-boniface-viii">Pope Boniface VIII</a> in 1298. His feast day is 28 August, also kept as a lesser festival in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a>. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>'s True Martyrology his body was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cagliari">Cagliari</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> by Catholic bishops expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, and around 720 it was moved to Pavia. In 1695 workmen at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-pietro-in-ciel-doro">San Pietro in Ciel d'Oro</a> found a box of bones, which the Bishop of Pavia declared to be Augustine's. In 1842 part of his right arm was returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annaba">Annaba</a>.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Augustine of Hippo&#8221; cites where it covers Death and sainthood:</p><ul><li>Harold Samuel Stone, St. Augustine's Bones: A Microhistory (University of Massachusetts Press, 2002)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jerome"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jerome.jpg" alt="Jerome" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jerome">Jerome</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MatthiasStom-SaintJerome-Nantes.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Matthias Stom, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Augustine is a saint in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Eastern Orthodox Church</a>, the Lutheran churches and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglicanism">Anglican Communion</a>, and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambrose">Ambrose</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome">Jerome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-i">Gregory the Great</a> is one of the four Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-christendom">Latin Church</a> Fathers. He was the most cited author in the Summa Theologiae of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas">Thomas Aquinas</a>. Many Protestants, especially Calvinists and Lutherans, consider him one of the theological fathers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> because of his teaching on grace, though the Catholic Church disputes this reading. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodoxy">Eastern Orthodoxy</a> he is venerated, while some of his teachings, among them the filioque, are more disputed.</p><p>The historian Diarmaid MacCulloch wrote that his impact on Western Christian thought "can hardly be overstated". His account of time in the Confessions drew Edmund Husserl's praise, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannah-arendt">Hannah Arendt</a> wrote her first dissertation on his concept of love; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-wittgenstein">Ludwig Wittgenstein</a> opened Philosophical Investigations with a passage from the Confessions. According to Leo Ruickbie, his arguments against magic later became central to the denunciation of witchcraft.</p><p>His views on sexuality and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> remain debated. He wrote that slavery was a result of sin and led clergy under him to free their slaves, but he also held it to be penal and urged slaves to serve in faithful love. He taught that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> should not be slain or forcibly converted, because their scriptures bear witness to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, a view that became Church policy; the saying that they should "survive but not thrive" is not found in his writings.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Augustine of Hippo&#8221; cites where it covers Legacy, In theology, Oratorio, music:</p><ul><li>Frank Leslie Cross, The Oxford Dictionary of the Christian Church (University Press, 2005)</li></ul></details>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Augustine of Hippo (354–430), born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thagaste">Thagaste</a> in Numidia, taught rhetoric, passed through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manichaeism">Manichaeism</a> and was baptised at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> in 387. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Hippo he wrote the Confessions and The City of God and shaped Western teaching on grace and original sin.</em></p>

